00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.620
Ecorobotics, a Cambridge, Massachusetts startup founded in 2024,

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:09.020
has raised a $150 million Series A at a $1.45 billion post-money valuation.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:12.260
Andresen Horowitz and F-Prime led the round.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:17.200
ECA is led by MIT, Professor Polkett Agraw and Chuomas Harnoja,

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:19.320
who used to be at Google DeepMind.

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:24.260
Its product is a set of robotic systems driven by what it calls a vision force

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:25.000
action model.

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:29.140
ECA says it does about 1,500 pics per hour.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:31.680
It puts a human worker at around 600.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:36.100
No customer has been named, and no production deployment has been described.

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:40.760
So a Series A is being priced on numbers the company reports about itself.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:42.500
Eka is not alone.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:46.420
Physical intelligence is reportedly valued at $11 billion,

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:51.420
and generalist at roughly twice Eka's $1.45 billion.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:56.940
The story is that the recipe that worked for text will work for robots.

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:00.560
More data, bigger models, predictable gains.

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:03.380
That recipe deserves a hard look now,

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:06.080
before more capital is committed on the strength of it.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:09.920
Start with why scale worked for text.

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:13.160
Language models learned from a library people had already written.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:16.440
Trillions of words were sitting on the internet, free to collect.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.040
Make the model bigger, feed it more text,

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:21.800
and its errors fell along a smooth, predictable curve.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:24.140
Investors could plan around that curve.

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:26.260
Robots don't have that library.

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:29.500
Each useful example is a record of a physical action.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.180
What the camera saw, what the arm did, what the fingers felt, and whether it worked.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:37.320
Someone has to produce every one of those records.

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:39.200
There are three ways to do it.

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:44.720
The first is teleoperation, where a person remote controls a robot and every movement is logged.

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.900
That gives high-quality data, but it is slow and you pay for every hour.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:53.840
The second is simulation, where a physics engine runs millions of virtual attempts.

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:58.160
That is cheap and fast, but the virtual world is never quite the real one.

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:02.360
Friction, soft materials, and glare all come out slightly wrong.

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:06.780
The third is human video, which exists in huge amounts,

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:09.000
but shows only what happened, not how it felt.

00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:11.200
Here's the analogy to keep in mind.

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:15.020
Picture a teenager who has watched 10,000 hours of driving videos.

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:17.440
They know what parallel parking looks like.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:20.020
Put them in a real car, and they still clip the curb,

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:23.020
because the feel of the pedal and the wheel was never in the footage.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:25.920
Robot manipulation is mostly that feel.

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:30.300
It is how hard to squeeze when something slips when a screw has caught its thread.

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:34.100
That is why Eka puts force at the center of its pitch.

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:39.180
It says it builds what it calls an intuition of physics through reinforcement learning at scale.

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:44.080
In plain words, the robot practices by trial and error in simulation,

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:47.260
gets rewarded when it succeeds, and adjusts.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:52.140
In Eka's words, robots should master physics, not mimic human data.

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:56.980
The open question is whether that practice carries over to a real factory floor.

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:00.700
Some of this is genuinely established.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:06.260
In March, a team published MalmoBot, a model trained only on simulated data.

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:11.600
They released MalmoBot data, a data set of 1.8 million expert trajectories

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:14.860
for articulated object manipulation and pick-and-place tasks.

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:17.120
With no real-world training at all,

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:22.480
it reached 79.2% success in real tabletop tests across four settings.

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:26.760
The comparison model they tested scored 39.2%.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:31.220
That is a real result in a paper against a named baseline.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:37.800
A second paper, Deximtoreal, reports a 78.2% average real-world success

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:40.460
rate across six challenging manipulation tasks.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:42.480
Its testing was blinded.

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:45.360
So simulation can now take a robot from nothing

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:47.780
to working most of the time on bounded tasks.

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:50.220
Notice what most of the time means, though.

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:52.840
On scaling, the evidence is thinner.

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:57.360
A 2024 paper by Sebastian Sartor and Neil Thompson

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:59.960
titled Neural Scaling Laws in Robotics

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:02.660
examined how scaling laws apply to robotics.

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:05.700
It found robot performance improving with data,

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:08.680
model size, and compute along power law curves.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:10.200
That is suggestive.

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:12.680
But a meta-analysis across different labs,

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:17.040
robots and tasks is a weaker kind of evidence than one controlled experiment.

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:22.660
The strongest scaling claim comes from Generalist about its own model, Gen Zero.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:28.320
It says it trained on over 270,000 hours of real manipulation data,

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:30.640
growing by 10,000 hours a week.

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:34.040
It reports a power law link between how much it portrays

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:35.880
and how well the model does afterwards.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:40.960
It says models below about 7 billion parameters struggle to absorb the data.

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:43.020
Those are interesting findings.

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:47.340
They are also self-reported, measured mostly as prediction error,

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:49.220
and not independently replicated.

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:50.800
Then there is ECA.

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:55.640
The 1,500 pics per hour figure comes from the company.

00:15:56.260 --> 00:16:00.960
We found no published method, no description of the objects or the test conditions,

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:02.760
and no outside audit.

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:06.700
One analysis of the round put the next proof point plainly.

00:16:07.220 --> 00:16:10.920
It is a named customer running the robots on a production line for months,

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:18.680
not minutes. Facts allow more than one honest reading. The AGI forward reading goes like this.

00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:24.240
Text models also looked brittle before scale. Now there are smooth curves from generalist and in

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:29.620
the meta-analysis. Simulation results jumped within a year, and data collection is speeding up.

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:35.700
Andresen Horowitz, which led Eka's round, is plainly in this camp. It's Raghu Ragyuram called

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:40.720
Eka's model the first approach he had seen that truly solves manipulation with the generality and

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:46.340
throughput deployments need. The AA Realist reading starts from the same facts and lands

00:16:46.340 --> 00:16:52.000
somewhere else. Rodney Brooks, who co-founded iRobot, is particularly skeptical of companies

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:57.300
like Tesla and Figure trying to teach robots dexterity by showing them videos of humans doing

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:03.640
tasks. In his words, we don't have such a tradition for touch data. Speech and vision had decades of

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:09.500
collected data behind them. Touch has very little. A Realist also notes that falling prediction error

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:11.560
is not the same as fewer dropped parts.

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:14.540
And the last few percent of reliability

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:17.200
is usually where robotics projects die.

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:19.200
The market reading is narrower.

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:21.600
Buyers don't pay for generality.

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:24.400
They pay for uptime on a specific line,

00:17:24.840 --> 00:17:26.380
where the readings agree is simple.

00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:28.960
Nobody has yet shown, in public,

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:30.960
a general manipulation model

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:33.300
holding up over months in a customer's plant.

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:37.520
Our read is that simulation-trained manipulation

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:39.480
is real and improving fast.

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:42.700
But the scaling story for robots is still a hypothesis,

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:45.580
backed mostly by companies measuring themselves.

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:47.900
That changes what can be built now.

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:52.880
Bounded jobs look within reach, picking, kitting, simple assembly,

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:55.480
with fixed objects and forgiving tolerances.

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:59.140
Open-ended dexterity in messy settings does not yet.

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:01.600
Serving costs are different from software.

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:05.940
Each deployment brings hardware, integration work,

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:07.440
sensors that wear out,

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:09.880
and someone to step in when the robot fails.

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:14.680
Margins depend on how often that person is needed, not only on the model.

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:19.680
For a seed or Series A company, the edge may not be owning the biggest model.

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:24.360
It may be owning a workflow, the data that comes back from real deployments,

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:26.000
and the customer relationship.

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:29.160
The exposure is the reverse case,

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:32.880
a thin application layer built on someone else's robot model,

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:34.540
with no data of its own.

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:38.820
One question worth exploring in diligence might be the intervention rate.

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:43.620
How many times per hour or per shift does a person have to step in

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:46.700
on a real customer line measured over weeks?

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:50.780
We'd also want the success rate on objects the system has never seen

00:18:50.780 --> 00:18:53.660
and the method behind any picks per hour figure.

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:57.640
Three things would settle a lot of this.

00:18:58.180 --> 00:19:01.920
First, a named customer publishing uptime and intervention data

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:05.080
after months of production from ECA or any of its peers.

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:08.320
Its own analysts say that is the next proof point,

00:19:08.620 --> 00:19:09.840
so it could come within a year.

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:14.080
Second, independent replication of a robot scaling curve.

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:17.160
That would mean a lab with no commercial stake showing

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:21.200
that more data improves real task success, not just prediction error.

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:25.400
Third, shared benchmarks for dexterity and force

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:29.600
run under blinded conditions, the way Deximtoreal was tested.

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:34.960
If those arrive and the curves hold, the AGI forward reading gains ground.

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:40.460
If deployments stall at the last few percent of reliability, the realists were right.

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:43.720
Here's Stephen's take.

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:49.800
Determining what information is explicitly embedded in models as part of the data ingestion process

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:53.020
and what is implicitly embedded is extremely challenging.

00:19:53.660 --> 00:19:57.140
And in most real-world atom-moving solutions, the gap is critical,

00:19:57.140 --> 00:20:02.660
and thus we need to have a very real show me mentality for what is a fantastic demo

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:07.500
and what is a realistic in production approach and this is very problem specific

